1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:01,920
Did you know that some Japanese

2
00:00:01,940 --> 00:00:03,930
kanji started as pictures?

3
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:05,600
Thousands of years ago,

4
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:06,840
people needed a way to

5
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:08,480
record the world around them.

6
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:10,160
In ancient China, people

7
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:11,760
carved symbols onto oracle

8
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:13,600
bones and turtle shells.

9
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:15,360
Some of these early characters

10
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:16,160
represented things they

11
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:18,080
could see in everyday life.

12
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:18,960
The sun.

13
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:19,920
The moon.

14
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:20,880
Mountains.

15
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:21,770
Trees.

16
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:23,360
Look at this character:

17
00:00:23,380 --> 00:00:25,360
"hi", meaning sun.

18
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:27,160
Early forms resembled a drawing of

19
00:00:27,180 --> 00:00:29,680
the sun, often with a mark inside.

20
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:31,440
Over centuries, its shape

21
00:00:31,460 --> 00:00:32,880
changed through different writing

22
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:34,600
styles, until it became the

23
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:36,400
character we recognize today.

24
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:38,560
And the idea doesn't stop there.

25
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:40,400
"Yama" means mountain.

26
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:42,090
"Kawa" means river.

27
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:43,920
"Ki" means tree.

28
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:45,600
Look closely, and you can see

29
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:46,880
how some of these characters

30
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:48,890
resemble the things they represent.

31
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:51,040
But here's the fascinating part:

32
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:53,200
not every kanji is a picture.

33
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:55,240
Many characters combine a component

34
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:56,880
related to meaning, with another

35
00:00:56,900 --> 00:00:58,890
that gives a clue to pronunciation.

36
00:00:58,980 --> 00:01:00,000
This allowed the writing

37
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:01,440
system to express far

38
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:03,360
more than simple objects.

39
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:04,640
Over time, Chinese

40
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:06,000
characters developed into a

41
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:08,000
sophisticated writing system.

42
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:09,640
They later reached Japan through

43
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:11,400
historical cultural contacts,

44
00:01:11,420 --> 00:01:12,560
and were adapted to write

45
00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:14,000
the Japanese language.

46
00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:15,200
These characters became

47
00:01:15,220 --> 00:01:17,290
known in Japanese as kanji.

48
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:18,320
So the next time you

49
00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:20,200
write a kanji, remember:

50
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:21,840
behind that single character

51
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:24,080
may lie centuries of history.

52
